    "analysis": "<p>React Native's JavaScript Interface (JSI) was a revolutionary step away from the JSON-serialized asynchronous bridge, enabling direct C++ communication between the JavaScript runtime (Hermes or JSC) and native modules. While JSI inherently eliminates JSON serialization/deserialization overhead for basic types and primitive operations, complex data structures or frequent, large data transfers still incur marshaling/unmarshaling costs between JS values and C++ types. More critically, JSI's synchronous capabilities, while powerful, introduce a new class of concurrency issues: deadlocks. If a native module makes a synchronous JSI call back to JavaScript while holding a native lock that the JavaScript thread also needs to acquire (e.g., to access a shared resource or fulfill a callback), a deadlock can occur. This freezes the application, as both threads indefinitely wait for each other, leading to a unresponsive UI and a poor user experience. This problem is exacerbated in scenarios involving complex threading models or tightly coupled native and JS logic.</p>",
    "root_cause": "Excessive synchronous JSI calls from native code to JavaScript that block the JavaScript thread. A common scenario for deadlocks is a native module acquiring a lock on a shared resource (e.g., a mutex protecting a data structure) and then making a synchronous JSI call back to the JavaScript thread. If the JavaScript thread, to process this call or another event, also attempts to acquire the *same* native lock (perhaps via another native module call or a callback mechanism), a circular waiting condition arises, leading to a deadlock.",
    "bad_code": "// Example scenario: A native module (C++) locks a resource and then calls JS synchronously\n// to get some value, but JS needs the same resource to return the value.\n\n// In Native Module (C++):\nstd::mutex g_native_mutex;\n\njsi::Value NativeModule::syncCallToJS(jsi::Runtime &rt, const jsi::Value &arg) {\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(g_native_mutex); // Acquire native lock\n    // ... perform some native work ...\n\n    // BAD: Synchronous call to JS while holding a native lock.\n    // If the JS function also needs to acquire g_native_mutex (e.g., via another native call),\n    // a deadlock will occur.\n    jsi::Function jsFunc = rt.global().getPropertyAsFunction(rt, \"getJSData\");\n    return jsFunc.call(rt, arg);\n}\n\n// In JavaScript (App.js):\nconst NativeModule = global.NativeModule; // Assuming NativeModule is exposed via JSI\n\nfunction getJSData() {\n  // Imagine this function internally triggers another native call\n  // that tries to acquire g_native_mutex. E.g.,\n  // NativeModule.anotherNativeCallWhichNeedsMutex();\n  return \"data from JS\";\n}\n\n// This will likely deadlock if getJSData() requires g_native_mutex implicitly or explicitly.\nNativeModule.syncCallToJS(\"input\");",
    "solution_desc": "Design native modules with an asynchronous philosophy. Avoid synchronous JSI calls from native to JavaScript, especially when holding native locks. Instead, use callbacks or Promises to return results from JavaScript back to native code. Offload heavy or potentially blocking native operations to background threads (e.g., using `dispatch_async` on iOS or `AsyncTask` on Android) to keep the main JS thread responsive. Implement robust thread-safe mechanisms for shared resources, ensuring that locks are released before making calls that might re-enter the JavaScript context or trigger other native calls that could contend for the same locks. For critical sections, consider lock-free algorithms or event-driven patterns.",
    "good_code": "// In Native Module (C++):\nstd::mutex g_native_mutex;\n\n// Use asynchronous callbacks to avoid blocking.\nvoid NativeModule::asyncCallToJS(jsi::Runtime &rt, const jsi::Value &arg, jsi::Function callback) {\n    std::thread([&rt, arg = std::move(arg), callback = std::move(callback)]() {\n        // Perform potentially blocking native work here on a background thread.\n        // If g_native_mutex is needed, acquire and release it within this scope.\n        {\n            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(g_native_mutex); // Acquire, perform work, release.\n            // ... critical native work ...\n        }\n\n        // Once native work is done, transition back to the JS thread to call the callback.\n        // Assuming an executor for the JS thread (e.g., React Native's Queue/RuntimeScheduler)\n        // This is pseudo-code for a safe JS thread dispatch.\n        rt.global().getPropertyAsFunction(rt, \"setTimeout\").call(\n            rt, jsi::Function::createFromHostFunction(rt, jsi::PropNameID::forAscii(rt, \"callback\"), 0, \n                [&rt, cb = std::move(callback), result = jsi::String::createFromAscii(rt, \"data from native\")]\n                (jsi::Runtime& innerRt, const jsi::Value& thisVal, const jsi::Value* args, size_t count) -> jsi::Value {\n                    cb.call(innerRt, result);\n                    return jsi::Value::undefined();\n                }\n            ), 0\n        );\n\n    }).detach(); // Detach the thread, or manage its lifecycle properly.\n}\n\n// In JavaScript (App.js):\nconst NativeModule = global.NativeModule;\n\nfunction handleNativeResult(result) {\n  console.log(\"Received from native: \", result);\n  // No need to worry about deadlocks here, as the native call was async.\n}\n\n// Asynchronous call from JS, providing a callback.\nNativeModule.asyncCallToJS(\"input\", handleNativeResult);",
